desc(
	mode: "source"
	language: "general"
	title: "Spring Actuator Fully Enabled"
	title_zh: "Spring Actuator Fully Enabled"
	type: vuln
	level: high
	rule_id: "source-insecure-api-spring-actuator-fully-enabled"
	risk: "information-exposure"
	desc: <<<DESC
Migrated from rule `spring-actuator-fully-enabled` (java/spring/security/audit/spring-actuator-fully-enabled.yaml).
Original message: Spring Boot Actuator is fully enabled. This exposes sensitive endpoints such as /actuator/env, /actuator/logfile, /actuator/heapdump and others. Unless you have Spring Security enabled or another means to protect these endpoints, this functionality is available without authentication, causing a significant security risk.
CWE: CWE-200: Exposure of Sensitive Information to an Unauthorized Actor
References:
- https://docs.spring.io/spring-boot/docs/current/reference/html/production-ready-features.html#production-ready-endpoints-exposing-endpoints
- https://medium.com/walmartglobaltech/perils-of-spring-boot-actuators-misconfiguration-185c43a0f785
- https://blog.maass.xyz/spring-actuator-security-part-1-stealing-secrets-using-spring-actuators
Note: source-mode regex approximation of a safe-literal pattern.
Original paths.include: ['*properties'].
DESC
	solution: <<<SOLUTION
Review this occurrence and replace it with a safer alternative.
SOLUTION
	alert_min: 1
	"file://pos.properties": <<<POS
management.endpoints.web.exposure.include=*
POS
	"safefile://pos.properties": <<<NEG
management.endpoints.web.exposure.include=health
NEG
)

${*.properties}.pattern_regex(/(?:management\.endpoints\.web\.exposure\.include=\*)/) as $hit
alert $hit for {
	level: "high",
	title: "Spring Actuator Fully Enabled",
	title_zh: "Spring Actuator Fully Enabled",
	message: "Spring Actuator Fully Enabled detected.",
}
